{% if section_type == "table_of_contents" %}
## TABLE OF CONTENTS REVIEW (MANDATORY)
- Every TOC entry on every consecutive TOC page, including continuation pages, must show its title at the left, its terminal Arabic or Roman page number at the far right, and a flexible dotted leader filling the middle from near the title to near the number. Every terminal page number must share one vertical right edge, including indented subsection rows; hierarchy indentation belongs on title-side padding and must not shift the whole row. Reject any rendering where the number remains appended to the title, appears in a left-hand column, the dots occur after the number, or the leader is missing or short.
- The decorative leader must be an EMPTY flexible element painted with a dotted border. Reject a finite visible string such as `"................................"`; it leaves a blank gap before the number at wide viewports.
- OCR may merge title and page number into one text leaf (`"Digestive system1"`, `"Introduction vi"`). For this section only, preserve its one parent `data-id` while using child spans marked `data-toc-title="true"` and `data-toc-page-number="true"`, plus an `aria-hidden` flexible span marked `data-toc-leader="true"` between them. The parent's combined text content must remain exactly equal to the source leaf; the markers let runtime translation preserve the row layout. When title and page number are separate leaves, preserve both `data-id` elements as siblings around the empty leader.
- Preserve chapter/section/subsection indentation from the source page. Do not remove a correct dotted-leader row during review.
{% endif %}

## IMPORTANT: SECTION SCOPE
The screenshots show a **single section**, which is typically only a SUBSET of the original page — not the whole page. A page is usually composed of multiple sections (a header, a body paragraph, an image with caption, a sidebar, etc.), and you are only reviewing ONE of them at a time.

- **Locate the corresponding region** in the original page image that matches this section's content (by matching the text, images, and headings present in the rendered screenshot). Compare ONLY against that region — do NOT expect the rendered section to reproduce the full page.
- The rendered section will typically occupy the full viewport width, even if in the original it only takes up a column, a strip, or a small portion of the page. This is correct and expected — do NOT reject for this.
- Other content visible in the original page image (other sections, headers, footers, page numbers, surrounding text, neighboring images) is NOT part of this section and MUST NOT appear in the rendering. Do NOT flag missing content that belongs to a different section.
- Match layout, hierarchy, alignment, and styling against the corresponding region only. For example, if the section is a two-column block within a larger page, compare its column structure to that block — not to the page as a whole.

