## TEXTBOOK IMAGE-INTEGRITY REVIEW
- **The current HTML is the authority for image admission.** The generator has already triaged every image candidate. A source-page image node that is absent from the current HTML was intentionally omitted.
- Image membership is monotonic during review: a correction MAY remove an existing `<img>`, but MUST NEVER add one, restore one removed in an earlier iteration, change an image `data-id`, or invent/change its `src`. Every corrected image set must be a subset of the current HTML's image set.
- Generated crop membership and its stable coordinate system are immutable during review. Never remove a `data-textbook-crop="true"` wrapper or its `<img>`. You MAY tighten the crop after screenshot comparison, but must recompute the wrapper `aspect-[W/H]` plus the image percentage `w-[…%]`, `left-[…%]`, and `top-[…%]` together from one revised native-image rectangle. Keep `relative overflow-hidden` on the wrapper and `absolute max-w-none h-auto` on the image. Never use inline styles, `object-cover`, `object-position`, `h-full`, `w-full`, or breakpoint-specific crop coordinates. State the revised approximate rectangle in your reasoning.
- `sr-only` transcription decisions are monotonic: you MAY add `sr-only` to a proven baked-text duplicate, but MUST NEVER remove it from a text `data-id` that already has it.
- Never report an image as missing merely because it appears in the content tree or original page. If its `<img>` is absent from the current HTML, review the semantic adaptation without it.
- Reject a full or near-full page raster rendered visibly as a whole inside this section. A page-sized source is acceptable only inside a `data-textbook-crop="true"` exact-aspect-ratio wrapper that reveals one tight, essential figure and never exposes adjacent printed content at any viewport. Reject crop leakage or fixed-height/object-position geometry. Tighten a leaking crop only with one newly calculated invariant rectangle as described above; if you cannot calculate that safely, return empty correction content rather than removing the crop.
- Crop leakage includes even a clipped fragment of a caption, instruction, heading, paragraph, answer line, or neighboring section at an edge. Do not approve a crop merely because most of the duplicate text is hidden.
- Reject a visible worksheet/form composite when the HTML also reconstructs its instructions, questions, table, or controls. Keep the semantic HTML and remove the duplicate raster composite.
- Reject visible duplicate labels: if a retained figure already contains a legible label and the same supplied leaf is drawn again, keep the essential figure and make only the matching transcription leaf `sr-only`. This includes baked-in `a)`, `b)`, and `c)` labels on activity option figures.
- For activities, keep instructions, questions, choices, tables, and controls visible as semantic HTML. An individual evidence/option figure may remain when it is needed to answer; hide only its exact duplicate transcription leaves, never the controls or prompt.
- For every retained activity figure, compare the text leaves in its `image_group` against the pixels. Reject visible duplicates of baked option markers, card/form titles, printed labels, prices, units, and captions; make only those exact transcription leaves `sr-only`.
- When a retained figure contains a printed blank/line/box, reject a semantic input duplicated below or beside the image. The semantic control must overlay that exact writable area: position it absolutely inside one `relative` wrapper with the image's exact aspect ratio and percentage coordinates shared by all viewports.
- Reject empty image stand-ins: a blank image-sized panel, skeleton, empty aspect-ratio box, or fabricated CSS illustration/card grid left where generation omitted an image. Remove the stand-in rather than pretending the source visual was reconstructed.
- An anchored overlay is valid only when the anchor is a section-level figure and its image plus overlays share one exact aspect-ratio wrapper. Reject coordinate drift caused by positioning against a larger container or letterboxed image.
